In this level I had to debug `/challenge/reverse-me` in GDB and find which input character leads to the success path, by examining a jump table the binary uses instead of a normal conditional branch.

I ran `x/256a 0x401000` to dump a chunk of memory as an array of addresses and scanned through it looking for the one entry that pointed somewhere different from all the rest, that's the success case, sitting at address `0x401228`.


My first attempt was computing the index as `(0x401228 - 0x401000) / 8`,
treating `0x401000` as the table's base since that's where I'd started my
memory dump. That gave index 69, which as ASCII is `'E'`, it didn't work.


The actual mistake was assuming "where I started dumping memory" was the
same as "where the table actually starts." I'd dumped a wider window than
the table itself, so `0x401000` was memory *before* the table, not the
table's base. The real base was `0x401088`.


Redoing it as `(0x401228 - 0x401088) / 8` gave index 52, which is ASCII
`'4'`. Running `/challenge/reverse-me 4` gave me the flag.
